#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# wigner_d

#% text
# `wigner_d([j, m1, m2], theta)` gives the Wigner small d function; `wigner_d([j, m1, m2], theta, phi)` and `wigner_d([j, m1, m2], psi, theta, phi)` the D function with the phases `exp(i m2 phi)` and `exp(i m1 psi + i m2 phi)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `j` and the `m` are integers or half-integers with `|m| <= j`; the value is an exact trigonometric expression and numerical for numerical angles.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
wigner_d([1/2, 1/2, -1/2], t)
#% output
#= sin((1/2)*t)

#% cell
wigner_d([1, 0, 1], psi, t, phi)
#% output
#= -cos((1/2)*t)*2^(1/2)*exp(im*phi)*sin((1/2)*t)

#% cell
wigner_d([2, 2, 0], t)
#% output
#= 6^(1/2)*cos((1/2)*t)^2*sin((1/2)*t)^2

#% cell
n(wigner_d([1, 0, 1], 0.3, 0.5, 0.2))
#% output
#= -0.33224751865698443 - 0.06734990630462565im

